The sum of the first 98 terms of the arithmetic progression with common difference 1 is given as 137. Let \(O\) be the sum of the odd-indexed terms \(a_1, a_3, \dots, a_{97}\) and \(E\) be the sum of the even-indexed terms \(a_2, a_4, \dots, a_{98}\). Since each even term is one more than the preceding odd term, we have \(E = O + 49\). The total sum is \(O + E = 137\), so \(O + (O + 49) = 137\), giving \(2O = 88\) and \(O = 44\). Then \(E = 44 + 49 = 93\).

Thus, \(a_2 + a_4 + a_6 + \dots + a_{98} = 93\).

\boxed{93}

